{
  "schema": "fractal.project.v1",
  "project": {
    "slug": "fractal-self-healing-graph-benchmark",
    "title": "Fractal Self-Healing Graph Benchmark",
    "prompt": "Improve the Fractal source in /workspace/fractalmaster so its execution graph continuously coordinates and safely repairs itself, with measured proof that changes improve speed, reliability, and efficiency.\n\nPreserve compatibility with .fractal/project.fractal and existing projects. Implement:\n\n- Self-healing coordination for dead or missing coordinators, stale worker checkouts, abandoned locks, interrupted transitions, partial local/remote synchronization, duplicate receivers, orphaned assignments, completed or disappeared workers. Recovery must be idempotent and dependency-safe, with no duplicate ownership or completion.\n- Autonomous scheduling that keeps dependency-ready work moving, chains assignments after completion, restores parallel lanes after interruption, and requests bounded non-overlapping graph expansion when available parallelism is genuinely insufficient.\n- A deterministic benchmark and fault-injection harness comparing baseline and candidate behavior across linear, wide-parallel, diamond, mixed-wave, and failure-injected graphs.\n- Metrics for throughput, p50/p95 assignment latency, coordinator recovery time, stranded tasks, duplicate assignments, dependency violations, completion rate, retries, sync conflicts, graph-expansion quality, and practical command/CPU/memory overhead.\n- One repeatable local/CI benchmark command, machine-readable results, human-readable before/after reports, correctness gates, statistically meaningful regression thresholds, and honest neutral or negative outcomes.\n- A bounded evidence-driven learning loop stored compactly in portable graph learning data, with safe rollback and no ability to weaken tests, dependency rules, acceptance criteria, or evidence requirements.\n- Automatic canonical-state convergence for worker join, project continuation, status, and graph board operations, with actionable diagnostics only when safe repair is impossible.\n- Unit, integration, stress, and fault-injection tests for multi-worker joins, coordinator termination/restart, completion chaining, stale-state recovery, synchronization races, and graph completion.\n- Full Rust formatting, tests, Clippy with warnings denied, and relevant macOS tests if touched.\n- A final before/after benchmark report identifying remaining bottlenecks and highest-value next experiments.\n\nUse Fractal's execution graph to create parallel-safe implementation, resilience, benchmarking, observability, and verification lanes. Preserve unrelated user changes. Never manually mutate Fractal-owned graph state.",
    "visibility": "private"
  },
